---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { faqPageSchema } from "~/lib/structured-data";
import { SITE_URL } from "~/lib/og";
import { PUBLISHED_VERSION, SOURCE_VERSION } from "~/lib/site-facts.generated";

const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}/compare/stack-vs-pulumi/`;

const faq = faqPageSchema(
  [
    {
      question: "Does Stack replace Pulumi?",
      answer:
        "Stack coordinates supported provider setup and Phantom credential storage. Supabase, Neon, Turso and Vercel have resource-creation paths; other adapters attach to existing resources or accept credentials. Compare Pulumi's current documentation against your infrastructure requirements rather than treating this page as a vendor feature audit.",
    },
    {
      question: "Can I use Pulumi and Stack together?",
      answer:
        "You can evaluate separate workflows, but this source does not establish a native Pulumi-to-Stack or Phantom-to-ESC bridge. Qualify credential delivery, configuration and permissions for each tool before combining them.",
    },
    {
      question: "Why doesn't Stack just use the Pulumi SaaS providers?",
      answer:
        "Stack's reviewed source offers provider setup, sequential credential and configuration writes, conditional MCP descriptors and available doctor checks. It does not prove Pulumi provider coverage or feature superiority.",
    },
  ],
  pageUrl,
);
---

<Docs
  title="Stack vs Pulumi"
  description="Evaluate infrastructure requirements alongside Stack's supported provider setup, credential storage and configuration workflows."
  eyebrow="Comparison"
  extraSchemas={[faq]}
>
  <h2>TL;DR</h2>
  <p>Compare Stack with <a href="https://www.pulumi.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pulumi</a> against the workflow you need. Stack coordinates provider setup: four adapters have resource-creation paths, while others attach to existing resources or accept credentials. Credential storage and <code>.mcp.json</code> updates are sequential and conditional; Stack does not automatically generate a filled <code>.env</code>. Check current Pulumi documentation for its supported infrastructure and integration contracts.</p>

  <h2>When to pick each</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Pick Pulumi when</th>
        <th>Pick Stack when</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Check current provider support for the cloud resources you need.</td>
        <td>You want coordinated setup for supported SaaS adapters.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Check environment, region, state and drift requirements.</td>
        <td>You want to evaluate <code>stack add supabase</code> with authorized provider and Phantom setup.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Verify the vendor’s current compliance and audit controls.</td>
        <td>You want source-defined CLI, MCP and plugin workflows.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Choose based on the resources, credentials and configuration your workflow actually needs. <code>stack add stripe</code> accepts an existing credential; it does not create a Stripe account. Qualify each adapter before relying on provider setup.</p>

  <h2>At-a-glance</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Capability</th>
        <th>Stack</th>
        <th>Pulumi</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Category</td>
        <td>SaaS control plane</td>
        <td>Check current product scope.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Primary surface</td>
        <td>CLI + MCP server + Claude Code plugin</td>
        <td>Check supported CLI and language interfaces.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>What it provisions</td>
        <td>39 catalog entries; four adapters have create-resource paths, others attach or accept credentials.</td>
        <td>Check current resource and provider coverage.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Describes state with</td>
        <td><code>.stack.toml</code> declarations plus local instance metadata; managed credential slots use Phantom.</td>
        <td>Check current state-storage and backend contracts.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Secrets handling</td>
        <td>Sequential Phantom credential writes; no automatic filled <code>.env</code> or safe-to-commit token guarantee.</td>
        <td>Check current secret-storage and delivery guarantees.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>AI surface</td>
        <td>
          <a href="/docs/recommend/">Native <code>stack recommend</code></a> + MCP tools + Claude Code plugin
        </td>
        <td>Check current agent and AI interfaces.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>OAuth automation</td>
        <td>Configured OAuth or PAT/API-key paths, depending on the adapter.</td>
        <td>Check credential prerequisites for the selected provider.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>License</td>
        <td>MIT source {SOURCE_VERSION}; recorded public npm {PUBLISHED_VERSION} is a separate artifact.</td>
        <td>Check current license and commercial terms.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Free tier</td>
        <td>MIT source; no future pricing guarantee.</td>
        <td>Check current pricing and limits.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Best for</td>
        <td>SaaS composition, agentic workflows, Claude Code users</td>
        <td>Evaluate against your infrastructure and compliance requirements.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Where they overlap</h2>
  <p>The overlap to evaluate is provider setup and configuration management. Stack has a 39-entry source catalog, but catalog membership does not mean every adapter creates a resource. Inspect the selected adapter and the other tool’s current documentation before choosing an integration.</p>

  <h2>Where Stack wins</h2>
  <p>Source-backed Stack capabilities to evaluate:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conditional resource creation.</strong> <code>stack add supabase</code> can create a project using the selected adapter and authorized account. Creation, credential storage and later configuration can fail separately; inspect the resulting state.</li>
    <li><strong>Adapter-specific login.</strong> Some adapters use configured OAuth paths; others accept PATs or API keys. Verification strength varies, and a configured OAuth app is a prerequisite for its flow.</li>
    <li><strong>MCP control plane.</strong> Reviewed source declares 19 tools that forward CLI output as text. <code>stack_recommend</code>, <code>stack_add</code> and <code>stack_doctor</code> have separate effects and failure boundaries. Check the installed client’s actual tool list.</li>
    <li><strong>Local configuration.</strong> Stack separates <code>.stack.toml</code> declarations from local instance metadata and uses Phantom for managed credential slots. Project-supplied metadata is not automatically safe to commit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where Pulumi wins</h2>
  <p>Requirements to check against current Pulumi documentation:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cloud resource requirements.</strong> Stack’s AWS and GCP adapters do not create VPCs, subnets, IAM roles or RDS databases. Check the other tool’s current provider support for those needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Environment and state requirements.</strong> Evaluate region, environment, drift and state-management requirements against actual installed interfaces. This page does not qualify a multi-tool deployment.</li>
    <li><strong>Compliance requirements.</strong> Verify current certifications, access controls and audit behavior directly with the vendor. Stack source and an MIT license do not establish those controls.</li>
    <li><strong>Existing workflows.</strong> Preserve a working deployment while evaluating changes. A Phantom-to-ESC credential bridge is not implemented or qualified by this Stack source.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Can I use both?</h2>
  <p>You can evaluate separate workflows. A possible division to qualify:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pulumi</strong> should be evaluated against the infrastructure resources, permissions and state requirements of your project.</li>
    <li><strong>Stack</strong> coordinates supported provider setup and configuration. It does not take ownership of every SaaS account or delete upstream resources on removal.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>No native hand-off to Pulumi ESC is established by this source. Review the <a href="/docs/phantom/">Phantom integration</a> and the installed interfaces for credential delivery. <code>stack doctor</code> reports available checks; unchecked services and a zero exit are not proof that the combined application works.</p>

  <h2>Migration and caveats</h2>
  <p>This page describes reviewed Stack source {SOURCE_VERSION}. Recorded public npm {PUBLISHED_VERSION} and native release binaries are separate artifacts. The <a href="/docs/roadmap/">roadmap</a> describes proposals, not installed capabilities. Browse the <a href="/docs/providers/">39-entry source catalog</a> and qualify the installed adapter you intend to use.</p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <h3>Does Stack replace Pulumi?</h3>
  <p>Stack coordinates supported provider setup and Phantom credential storage. Supabase, Neon, Turso and Vercel have resource-creation paths; other adapters attach to existing resources or accept credentials. Compare Pulumi's current documentation against your infrastructure requirements rather than treating this page as a vendor feature audit.</p>

  <h3>Can I use Pulumi and Stack together?</h3>
  <p>You can evaluate separate workflows, but this source does not establish a native Pulumi-to-Stack or Phantom-to-ESC bridge. Qualify credential delivery, configuration and permissions for each tool before combining them.</p>

  <h3>Why doesn't Stack just use the Pulumi SaaS providers?</h3>
  <p>Stack’s reviewed source offers provider setup, sequential credential and configuration writes, conditional <a href="/docs/mcp/"><code>.mcp.json</code></a> descriptors and available doctor checks. It does not prove Pulumi provider coverage or feature superiority.</p>
</Docs>
